# STEP 5 — Baseline Observation (Normal vs Noise)

## Purpose

This step establishes the **normal operational baseline** for Iron Watch 02.
Before introducing any threat activity, the SOC must understand:

- What “normal” looks like
- What background noise exists
- How logs behave over time without attacks

This baseline becomes the **reference point** for all future detections.

---

## Scope

- Operation: Iron Watch 02
- Phase: Baseline observation
- SOC Node: soc-core03
- Log Sources:
    - soc-core03 (system & auth)
    - web-arm01 (Apache, auth, system)
- Detection logic: NONE
- Alerts: DISABLED
- Attacks: NONE

---

## Baseline Rules (Strict)

- No attacker activity
- No scanning
- No brute-force
- No fuzzing
- Only benign, intentional actions

If malicious activity occurs, **invalidate baseline and restart**.

---

## Observation Window

Recommended minimum:

- 30–60 minutes of normal operation

Optional:

- Repeat observations across different times (idle vs active)

---

## 1. SOC Node (soc-core03) — Baseline Behavior

### What to Observe

- SSH access patterns
- System messages
- Service behavior
- Log volume growth

---

### Commands

```bash
sudo tail -n 50 /var/log/syslog
sudo tail -n 50 /var/log/auth.log
```

Optional volume tracking:

```
sudowc-l /var/log/syslog
sleep60
sudowc-l /var/log/syslog
```

---

### Expected Normal Patterns

- Periodic system messages
- Occasional cron entries
- Minimal SSH activity
- No repeated authentication failures

📸 Evidence:

- Sample syslog entries
- Auth log snapshot

---

## 2. web-arm01 — Baseline Web Activity

### Generate Benign Traffic (Controlled)

On **web-arm01**, perform light, intentional actions:

```
curl http://localhost
curl http://localhost/index.html
curl http://localhost/nonexistent-page
```

Optional browser access from host machine.

---

### Observe Logs on soc-core03

```
sudogrep"web-arm01" /var/log/syslog | tail-n50
```

---

### Expected Normal Patterns

- HTTP 200 and 404 responses
- Single requests per action
- No request bursts
- Predictable User-Agent strings (curl, browser)

📸 Evidence:

- Apache access entries (via syslog)
- Error log entries for 404s

---

## 3. Authentication Noise Analysis

### What to Check

- SSH login attempts
- Failed vs successful auth
- Unexpected sources

---

### Commands

```
sudogrep"Failed password" /var/log/syslog
sudogrep"Accepted password" /var/log/syslog
```

---

### Expected Normal Patterns

- Few or zero failed attempts
- Known source IPs only
- No repeated failures

📸 Evidence:

- Auth-related log excerpts

---

## 4. Background Noise Identification

### Why this matters

Not all logs are attacks.

Some noise is environmental and must be understood.

---

### Commands

```
sudogrep-Ei"error|fail|warning" /var/log/syslog | head
```

Optional frequency check:

```
sudogrep-Ei"error|fail|warning" /var/log/syslog |wc-l
```

---

### Expected Normal Patterns

- Occasional warnings
- Non-repeating messages
- No escalation patterns

📸 Evidence:

- Sample warning/error entries

---

